.title = "Limits",
.description = "What HDR checks, what it cannot see, and where its evidence stops.",
.date = .date("2026-10-21T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.custom = { "section": "Reference", "prev": "cli" },
---

HDR is local governance, not a sandbox. It is only as strong as the harness's
willingness to call it.

# It cannot alert on its own absence

A harness that never runs the hook never hears from HDR. The `hdr_hooks` check
confirms that the checker is registered in the files HDR can read, which is not
the same as running:

- Codex skips project hooks in untrusted projects and any hook nobody has
  approved in `/hooks`. HDR cannot see that approval.
- Managed and server settings, launch flags, plugin-provided hooks and extra
  workspaces are not inspected.
- If the hook process crashes or times out, Claude Code and Codex decide what
  happens next. The opencode and pi plugins deny the tool call.

# Detection is not prevention

Shell commands are not parsed. A file changed through a shell, a subagent that
bypasses hooks, or another process is caught on the next hook by the session
baseline, after the change has happened. Restoring the file clears the failure.

A new session takes a fresh baseline from whatever is on disk. A change made
between sessions, or before `/clear` starts a new one, is accepted into the new
baseline.

# Evidence is local

Run reports and session baselines are plain files that any local process can
modify. They are useful evidence, not tamper-proof attestation. The checker's own
source and interpreter are not part of what HDR protects.

# Some harness behaviour is weaker

- opencode can only warn when a Stop check fails, and does not run plugin hooks
  for subagent tool calls.
- Codex enforcement has not yet been verified in a live session.
- `tool_denied` matches exact tool names in each harness's own vocabulary.

# Cost

Every hooked tool call starts a Python process twice, before and after the call.
Each run re-reads the decisions and settings and runs two quick Git commands.

# Verification of HDR itself

The test suite exercises real files, real Git history, CLI exit codes, hook
payloads for every harness, and the generated opencode and pi plugins under Node.
The repository's `scripts/check.sh` also runs Zanity, Ruff and strict type
checking.
